 \section{Discussion}
 
-The clearest results concern reliability, not accuracy. Post-hoc calibration removes most of the shipped
-baseline's overconfidence (79\% relative ECE reduction on both versions, controls excluded), and the gain does
-not depend on isotonic regression: Platt scaling and histogram binning do about as well. It improves the
-confidence users see, not the answers or their ranking. The decision-level result is that the hybrid's
-confidence orders its own errors better than the shipped one, on AURC, AUGRC, and correctness AUROC, on both
-versions and without the controls. OOD rejection is the third result, significant once the OOD subset grew
-from 15 to 50 queries, but its scope is narrower than the headline rate suggests: it comes mostly from
