 \section*{Ethical Considerations}
 
-TermAssist retrieves from a fixed, curated command library rather than generating arbitrary shell commands,
-which bounds its possible outputs to commands in that library. The library was checked by script rather than
-reviewed command by command (\S\ref{sec:system}), so this bound is weaker than full human vetting, and a
-retrieved command can still be inappropriate for a given user's intent; our own baseline audit found the
-deployed system is frequently \emph{confidently} wrong. We evaluate command risk with a separate,
-deterministic safety classifier (\S\ref{sec:ablation}) because retrieval correctness and command risk are
-orthogonal concerns, and we report that it misses at least one destructive command rather than presenting it
-as a safeguard. No component in this study executes a retrieved command against a user's real system: all
-functional-evaluation command execution ran in disposable, sandboxed temporary directories. We disclose the
-AI-assisted authorship of part of our benchmark (\S\ref{sec:system}) and the below-target result of our own
-partially independent re-annotation of it (\S\ref{sec:kappa}) rather than omit or minimize either. The code,
-benchmark files, result files, and a reproduction guide will be released with the camera-ready version; they
-are withheld from the review version for anonymity.
+The published tool runs the command it retrieves when the user presses Enter, and our results show it can offer a
+destructive command for a benign request, sometimes at maximum confidence. We report these cases rather than a safety
+claim, and recommend that such tools show a risk level and require explicit confirmation for high-risk commands. This
+study does not change the published package, so its findings apply to current users. No command was run on a user's
+system: functional evaluation ran in disposable sandboxed directories. We disclose that part of the benchmark was
+written by an AI agent and that our own label check fell below target. Code, benchmark files, result files and a
+reproduction guide will be released with the camera-ready version; they are withheld from the review version for
+anonymity.
 
